<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tawhid</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tawhid is the Islamic doctrine of the absolute oneness and unity of God, a central principle of Islamic theology. The reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a> placed an uncompromising interpretation of tawhid at the centre of his teaching. His followers called themselves al-Muwahhidun, the upholders of divine unity, from which the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> derives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tawhid.jpg" alt="Tawhid" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Kliczek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul,_Hagia_Sophia,_Allah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The word tawhid, an assertion or declaration of oneness, comes from the Arabic verb wahhada, which can mean to make one or to consider to be one. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> it holds that God is indivisibly one and single. The first part of the declaration of faith, the shahada, is the declaration of belief in the oneness of God, and Muslims believe that the whole of Islamic teaching rests on it. Chapter 112 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, al-Ikhlas, states that God is One, never had offspring and was not born, and has no equal. The Quran uses 99 terms, the Excellent Names of God, to express the complexity of the divine nature.</p><p>Its opposite is shirk, associating partners with God, a term usually translated as polytheism but understood to be more complex. It is classed as greater shirk, open and apparent, and hidden shirk, such as performing rituals for social recognition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> says that God does not forgive associating others with Him but forgives anything else of whoever He wills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Muslim theologians have argued for the oneness of God by reason. Against the polytheism of pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> argues that God as creator of everything excludes lesser gods, who would themselves have to be created, and that the order of the universe shows a single administrator. Verse 21:22 says that had there been gods besides God in the heavens and earth, both would have been ruined. Later theologians reasoned that two gods would sooner or later have conflicting wills, and one would be powerless, which is incompatible with being God. Avicenna (965–1037) proposed an ontological argument in the Metaphysics of The Book of Healing, distinguishing essence from existence.</p><p>Scholars have understood tawhid in different ways. Some theologians use it for the whole discussion of God and his attributes, and sometimes for the whole of kalam, Islamic theology. Philosophers such as al-Farabi, al-Razi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> interpreted it by reason, under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a>'s emphasis on divine simplicity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> it is both an affirmation in speech and a practical realisation of God's unity, through purification from worldliness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Tawhid shaped Muslim culture widely. Early Muslims understood religion to cover the state, law and society, since God's rule extends to all creatures and to all aspects of life. A single raised index finger is used as a symbol of tawhid. The wish to preserve God's transcendence led to the prohibition of visual depictions of God and of any prophet, and the dominant forms of Islamic art became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> and arabesque. The medieval scholar al-Ghazali held that a believer who thinks himself better than another through wealth or colour adopts the hierarchical principle of Iblis and so falls into shirk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tawhid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He included in such acts popular practices that made holy men intercessors with God, and in his Kitab al-<b>Tawhid</b> he denounced the veneration of trees and rocks.</blockquote><a href="/movement/wahhabism">Wahhabism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>It sees the ummah as the focus of allegiance and mobilisation, together with the belief in <b>Tawhid</b>, under the guidance of the Quran and the sunnah.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-islamism">Pan-Islamism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
